INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - LOBBY - MORNING

DESI OKONKWO, 20s, first-year associate, clutches a case file like it might escape. Partner MARGUERITE HALE, 50s, catches her by the elevator.

MARGUERITE
First solo hearing. Small claims, a security deposit dispute, nothing that ends a career either way it goes. Breathe.

DESI
I've read the file eleven times. I could recite the landlord's lease clauses in my sleep at this point.

MARGUERITE
Good. Now forget half of that and just listen to what actually happens in that room. The file never argues back. People do.

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - MARGUERITE'S OFFICE - MORNING

Marguerite pulls a chipped mug from a shelf lined with them, hands it to Desi.

MARGUERITE
My first solo hearing, I lost a slam-dunk case because I got so married to my outline I stopped listening to the judge's questions. Don't be me at twenty-six.

DESI
That is either very comforting or extremely alarming and I genuinely cannot tell which.

MARGUERITE
Both. Go. You've got twenty minutes to make the courthouse.

INT. COURTHOUSE - HALLWAY - MORNING

Desi checks the docket board when BRETT CALLOWAY, 30s, opposing counsel, falls into step beside her, entirely too pleased with himself.

BRETT
Okonkwo, right? Hargrove and Pyle's newest. I hear this is your first one solo.

DESI
Word travels fast around here. Good luck to you too, Mr. Calloway.

BRETT
I don't need luck for a security deposit dispute. But I appreciate the sentiment.

INT. COURTROOM - MORNING

Desi stands at counsel table, opening statement more halting than she rehearsed it. JUDGE PARETSKY watches over reading glasses, unreadable.

DESI
Your Honor, my client's deposit was withheld over damages that predate her tenancy by, ah — by several months, as this receipt from the prior tenant shows.

BRETT
Objection to the characterization, Your Honor. That receipt is dated the same week my client claims the damage occurred.

PARETSKY
Noted. Continue, counsel.

INT. COURTROOM - MORNING (CONTINUOUS)

Desi's client, ROSA IBARRA, takes the stand, glancing nervously at Desi between answers while Brett paces during cross.

BRETT
And you'd agree, Ms. Ibarra, that you never personally inspected the property before signing the lease?

ROSA
I trusted the listing photos. Isn't that the whole point of listing photos?

BRETT
That's not quite an answer to my question, is it.

Desi shuffles papers, searching for the receipt's exact filing date and not finding it fast enough.

INT. COURTHOUSE - HALLWAY - RECESS

Desi paces outside the courtroom, phone against her ear, getting Marguerite's voicemail instead of Marguerite. COURT CLERK ETTA MOSS passes with a cart of files.

ETTA
You're the one arguing the deposit case. That receipt Calloway's hammering on — there's a filing stamp on the back most people never flip it over to check.

DESI
Nobody mentioned a filing stamp to me. Where would that be logged?

ETTA
County recorder's index, third floor. You've got maybe fifteen minutes before recess ends.

INT. COURTHOUSE - LAW LIBRARY - RECESS

Desi flips through a bound index at speed, finger tracing down a column of dates until it stops.

DESI
(to herself)
There. Filed two weeks before the damage date, not the week of. Calloway had the receipt backwards the whole time.

She photographs the page and bolts for the stairs.

INT. COURTROOM - AFTERNOON

Desi rises for redirect, considerably steadier than she was that morning.

DESI
Your Honor, the recorder's index shows this receipt was filed two weeks before the alleged damage date, not the same week as opposing counsel suggested. I have the certified page here.

BRETT
(quietly, to his own table)
That's not what I was told this said.

PARETSKY
Approach with the exhibit, counsel.

INT. COURTROOM - AFTERNOON (CONTINUOUS)

Paretsky reviews the index page, then looks over her glasses at Brett, who has considerably less to say than he did that morning.

PARETSKY
I'm finding for the plaintiff on the deposit claim. The timeline opposing counsel argued simply doesn't hold up against the recorder's own filing stamp.

Rosa exhales audibly beside Desi, who allows herself one very small, very contained smile.

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - MARGUERITE'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Desi drops into the chair across from Marguerite, still buzzing.

MARGUERITE
I heard you found the filing stamp during recess. That's the kind of thing that doesn't come from an outline. That comes from actually listening in the room.

DESI
Etta from the clerk's office found it, technically. I just had the sense to ask her.

MARGUERITE
That counts double in my book. Knowing who to ask is half of this job.

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - BREAK ROOM - EVENING

Desi recounts the recorder's index discovery to two fellow associates, JORDY LANE and FEN ABIODUN, growing more animated with each retelling.

JORDY
You found a filing stamp on the back of an exhibit during a fifteen-minute recess. That's either genius or the luckiest Tuesday of your entire career.

DESI
Can it be both? I'd really like it to be both.

FEN
Marguerite's going to tell that story about you for the next decade. Consider yourself officially part of the firm folklore now.

EXT. HARGROVE & PYLE - STREET - EVENING

Desi steps out into the evening air, case file finally light in her hands instead of heavy.

DESI
(to herself)
Filing stamp on the back. Never would have thought to flip it over.

She pockets the file and heads for the train, already turning tomorrow's docket over in her head.
